  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Laws of Square Roots and Radical Arithmetic",
    "description": "Combine and simplify square roots using product, quotient, and like-radical rules.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Teach √(ab)=√a√b and √(a/b)=√a/√b for non-negative values with b>0, and explain why these rules do not permit √(a+b)=√a+√b. Simplify radicals by extracting square factors; combine like radicals only after simplification; preserve parentheses and operation order in expressions with sums, products, and quotients of roots. Include exact arithmetic with rational values and the chapter's multi-radical product/quotient patterns. Learners should select factors whose exponents contain pairs, simplify before calculating when useful, and verify by squaring or estimating."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Radical laws simplify products and quotients when their conditions hold. Simplify each root before combining terms, and never distribute a square root over addition.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For non-negative a and b, √(ab)=√a√b because squaring the right side returns ab and it is non-negative. Thus √(18×8)=√144=12. The quotient rule √(a/b)=√a/√b requires a≥0 and b>0.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
